---

<ToolPageShell
  slug="dog-pregnancy-calculator"
  title="Dog Pregnancy Calculator: Due Date"
  description="Estimate your dog’s whelping due date and window from breeding or ovulation date. Typical gestation is about 63 days."
  answer="A dog pregnancy calculator estimates the whelping date by adding about 63 days to the ovulation date, or to the breeding date with a wider window. Most litters arrive between day 58 and 68 from breeding. Ultrasound with your veterinarian confirms pregnancy and helps narrow the date."
  takeaways={[
    'Average gestation is about 63 days from ovulation.',
    'From breeding, whelping may occur anywhere from day 58 to 68.',
    'Ovulation timing (progesterone testing) gives a tighter estimate.',
    'Ultrasound can confirm pregnancy around day 25–28; X-ray counts puppies later.',
    'Call a vet immediately for prolonged labor, green discharge, or a dog past her window.',
  ]}
  faq={faq}
  sources={sources}
>
  <DogPregnancyCalc slot="calculator" />

  <h2>How to use the dog due date calculator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Choose whether your start date is a breeding date or a confirmed ovulation date.</li>
    <li>Enter the date. If there were several matings, use the first, or ideally the ovulation date from your vet.</li>
    <li>Read the estimated due date and the surrounding whelping window.</li>
    <li>Share the dates with your vet and plan ultrasound, X-ray, and a whelping area.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>How gestation is calculated</h2>
  <p>
    The standard figure is about 63 days from ovulation. That is the number that reproductive
    veterinarians use, because ovulation is the biological starting point. Breeding date is less
    exact. Female dogs ovulate over a span of a couple of days and the eggs need about two to three
    days to mature before they can be fertilized, while sperm may survive for several days in the
    reproductive tract. As a result, counting from the breeding date has a wide spread of about 58 to
    68 days.
  </p>
  <p>
    This calculator adds 63 days to the date you supply. When you choose breeding, it shows a window
    of plus or minus five days; when you choose ovulation, a tighter window of plus or minus two
    days. It does not replace progesterone testing, ultrasound measurements, or your veterinarian’s
    judgment.
  </p>

  <h2>Timeline of a typical dog pregnancy</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Approximate day</th><th>What is happening</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1–15</td><td>Fertilization and early embryo development; usually no visible signs.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>16–25</td><td>Implantation; some dogs have mild appetite or behavior changes.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>25–30</td><td>Ultrasound can confirm pregnancy and detect heartbeats.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>30–45</td><td>Weight gain begins; slightly enlarged abdomen and nipples.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>45–55</td><td>X-ray can count puppies; increase food under vet guidance.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>55–63</td><td>Nesting, restlessness; temperature drop 8–24 hours before labor.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Worked example</h2>
  <p>
    A bitch is bred on March 1 and ovulation is confirmed by progesterone testing on the same
    day. Using ovulation as the start date, the due date is 63 days later, on <strong>May 3</strong>,
    with a likely window of May 1 to May 5. If the owner did not test and only knows breeding
    happened on March 1, the window widens to about April 28 to May 8, with May 3 as the
    midpoint. Because the second range is wider, many breeders ask their vet for an X-ray around day
    55 to confirm litter size before the due date arrives.
  </p>

  <h2>Preparing for whelping</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Set up a quiet whelping box about two weeks before the due date, and let her get used to it.</li>
    <li>Take her rectal temperature twice daily from about day 58; a drop below 99°F (37.2°C) suggests labor within a day.</li>
    <li>Keep your vet’s number and an emergency clinic’s number available, including after-hours service.</li>
    <li>Feed a high-quality diet suited to pregnancy; see the <a href="/tools/puppy-food-calculator/">puppy food calculator</a> for later puppy feeding.</li>
    <li>Prepare clean towels, a heating pad, a scale, and a record sheet for each puppy’s birth time and weight.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to call the vet</h2>
  <p>
    Contact a veterinarian right away if there is green or bloody discharge before the first puppy,
    strong contractions for 30 to 60 minutes with no puppy, more than two to four hours between
    puppies when more are expected, a very weak or absent contraction pattern, or your dog is
    past about day 65 from ovulation. Dogs can lose puppies quickly in prolonged labor, and an
    emergency c-section can save the litter and the mother.
  </p>

  <h2>After the litter arrives</h2>
  <p>
    Newborn puppies should gain weight steadily from day one. You can track growth over the first
    weeks and months with the <a href="/tools/puppy-weight-calculator/">puppy weight calculator</a> and
    learn about long-term aging with the <a href="/tools/dog-age-calculator/">dog age calculator</a>.
    Breeding should always be planned with veterinary guidance, including health screening for the
    parents.
  </p>
</ToolPageShell>
